WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.749
Since Part 5, you have built one image, and changed how it behaves with environment variables.

2
00:00:08.749 --> 00:00:12.932
The same image runs on your laptop, in staging and in production.

3
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:14.889
Only the settings differ.

4
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.196
That is the twelve-factor rule.

5
00:00:17.196 --> 00:00:19.359
Store config in the environment.

6
00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:24.173
In Kubernetes, most teams put those settings in a ConfigMap.

7
00:00:24.173 --> 00:00:28.713
It is a small object, with a data section of keys and string values.

8
00:00:28.933 --> 00:00:30.798
The Deployment only names it.

9
00:00:30.798 --> 00:00:36.328
So the Deployment stays the same in every environment, and only the ConfigMap differs.

10
00:00:36.533 --> 00:00:38.451
It holds at most one mebibyte.

11
00:00:38.451 --> 00:00:43.949
And it is not secret at all: anyone who can read objects in the namespace can read it.

12
00:00:44.767 --> 00:00:48.741
A Pod can use a ConfigMap in three ways, and you can mix them.

13
00:00:48.967 --> 00:00:54.399
With env and configMapKeyRef, one key becomes one environment variable.

14
00:00:54.399 --> 00:00:56.617
You can rename it on the way.

15
00:00:56.833 --> 00:01:03.709
With envFrom and configMapRef, every key becomes an environment variable with the same name.

16
00:01:03.933 --> 00:01:09.145
The lab uses this, so GREETING and REDIS_HOST arrive as env vars.

17
00:01:09.367 --> 00:01:11.026
Or mount it as a volume.

18
00:01:11.026 --> 00:01:14.415
Every key becomes a file, in a folder you choose.

19
00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:17.993
Env vars suit short values your code reads at start.

20
00:01:17.993 --> 00:01:22.839
Files suit whole config files, and anything your code may read again later.

21
00:01:23.667 --> 00:01:26.314
The lesson's demo makes this visible.

22
00:01:26.314 --> 00:01:29.461
A ConfigMap, demo-config, holds COLOR: blue.

23
00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:35.092
A Pod reads that one value twice: as an env var, and as a file.

24
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:37.990
Every five seconds, it prints both.

25
00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:40.604
env equals blue, file equals blue.

26
00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:43.955
Now change the value in the ConfigMap to green.

27
00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:45.946
A ConfigMap is only data.

28
00:01:45.946 --> 00:01:47.725
No controller acts on it.

29
00:01:47.725 --> 00:01:50.501
So changing it never restarts anything.

30
00:01:50.733 --> 00:01:53.226
The kubelet reads it at two moments.

31
00:01:53.226 --> 00:01:56.549
When it starts a container, to fill in env vars.

32
00:01:56.767 --> 00:02:00.144
And on its regular sync, to refresh the mounted files.

33
00:02:01.167 --> 00:02:03.064
For a while, nothing changes.

34
00:02:03.064 --> 00:02:08.689
Then, on the kubelet's next sync, usually within a minute or so, the file shows green.

35
00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:10.886
The env var never changes.

36
00:02:10.886 --> 00:02:14.247
It was set once, when the container started.

37
00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:17.479
A file mounted with subPath is never updated.

38
00:02:17.479 --> 00:02:21.695
And even an updated file only helps if your app reads it again.

39
00:02:21.695 --> 00:02:24.172
Most apps read config once, at start.

40
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:30.635
So hello-devops, which uses env vars, needs new Pods to see a new greeting.

41
00:02:30.635 --> 00:02:34.016
The tool for that is kubectl rollout restart.

42
00:02:34.233 --> 00:02:38.229
It writes the current time into an annotation in the Pod template.

43
00:02:39.233 --> 00:02:43.549
The template changed, so the Deployment does a normal rolling update.

44
00:02:43.767 --> 00:02:46.205
Every new Pod starts with the new values.

45
00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:54.693
A Secret looks like a ConfigMap, but it is meant for sensitive values: passwords, API tokens, TLS keys.

46
00:02:54.693 --> 00:02:57.073
Pods use it the same three ways.

47
00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:00.933
Its values sit in data, encoded in base64.

48
00:03:01.167 --> 00:03:07.083
Base64 is a way to write any bytes as plain letters, so binary keys fit in YAML.

49
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:08.773
It is not encryption.

50
00:03:08.773 --> 00:03:11.580
Anyone can reverse it, with one command.

51
00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:15.645
It is like a note in an envelope marked confidential.

52
00:03:15.645 --> 00:03:18.619
Base64 is an envelope that is not sealed.

53
00:03:19.433 --> 00:03:23.280
A Secret does get some protection that a ConfigMap does not.

54
00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:27.821
It is sent only to nodes that need it, and kept in memory there.

55
00:03:27.821 --> 00:03:30.050
describe shows sizes, not values.

56
00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:33.696
And access rules can allow ConfigMaps but not Secrets.

57
00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:37.603
But by default, Secrets are stored unencrypted in etcd.

58
00:03:37.603 --> 00:03:43.663
And anyone who can create a Pod in a namespace can mount, and so read, every Secret in it.

59
00:03:44.467 --> 00:03:48.475
So turn on encryption at rest, and limit who can read Secrets.

60
00:03:48.475 --> 00:03:52.096
Limit who can create Pods, the step people often forget.

61
00:03:52.096 --> 00:03:54.876
And give each container only what it needs.

62
00:03:55.100 --> 00:04:01.785
A Secret manifest with real values never goes into git, where base64 is plain text forever.

63
00:04:01.785 --> 00:04:07.295
External Secrets Operator, Sealed Secrets or SOPS keep the real values out.

64
00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:10.408
Config lives outside the image.

65
00:04:10.408 --> 00:04:12.864
Env vars are read once, at start.

66
00:04:12.864 --> 00:04:15.098
Mounted files update in place.

67
00:04:15.098 --> 00:04:16.959
And base64 hides nothing.
